"Dockerd requires the unprivileged user namespace element (ns.userns_unpriv) to create isolated user namespaces for containers, mapping container UID/GID ranges to the host and enabling rootless or multi\u2011tenant containers without granting them real host privileges. This isolation prevents uid collisions and confines a compromised container to its own namespace.  \n\nFrom this surface an attacker gains a user\u2011namespace escape primitive (netfilter\u2011via\u2011userns or userns\u2011lpe) for host privilege escalation, an io_uring refcount use\u2011after\u2011free primitive allowing arbitrary kernel memory read/write, an io_uring use\u2011after\u2011free primitive with similar effect, a keyring use\u2011after\u2011free primitive that leaks or overwrites credential objects, and a keyctl refcount\u2011overflow primitive that corrupts reference counts leading to memory\u2011corruption\u2011based code execution.  \n\nDisable unprivileged user namespaces for dockerd, e.g., start it with --userns-remap=disabled or set kernel.user.max_user_namespaces=0, which removes ns.userns_unpriv usage. The tradeoff is that containers run in the host\u2019s UID/GID namespace, losing uid/gid isolation and increasing the impact of any container breakout on the host."